What:		/sys/bus/i2c/devices/<bus>-004a/touch_mode
Date:		October 2026
Description:
		Selects the stock volatile touch-controller configuration.
		Accepted values are "finger", "glove", "stylus" and
		"stylus_draw". Both pen modes use the same stock configuration.
		The default on driver probe is "finger". Reads return the last
		fully applied mode, followed by a newline. Only root can write.

		A mode change releases all active contacts and capacitive keys,
		writes the complete configuration and calibrates the controller.
		It never updates firmware or nonvolatile settings. While the
		display is blank or the system is suspended, a changed mode is
		remembered without I2C access and applied on resume. Reads return
		EIO until that new configuration has been applied.
		Unknown values return EINVAL.

		An incomplete configuration write, IRQ message read failure or
		controller reset/config fault stops touch reporting. Reads then
		return EIO; writing a valid mode while awake reapplies the entire
		configuration.
		A failed sleep/wake transition also stops reporting, but retains
		a completed configuration; writing its mode retries scanning.
		After a completed sleep, wake performs the stock soft reset and
		100ms wait, then restores the selected volatile configuration.
		Successful writes return the supplied byte count.

What:		/dev/touch_fops
Date:		October 2026
Description:
		Provides the S0226114 touch-mode ioctl interface, including
		read-only opens used by libsky_ctrl_drv.so. Command 6001 takes
		a scalar mode: 0 finger, 1 glove, 2 stylus, 3 stylus_draw.
		Command 6004 copies the selected mode to a userspace int pointer.
		Selection uses the same control path as the sysfs attribute.
		Command 6001 also accepts scalar 10 (normal screen) and 11
		(cover window). These leave the finger/glove/stylus selection
		unchanged. Command 6004 adds 10 when cover-window mode is selected.
		The cover window accepts Y 0 through 1250, matching S0226114.
		Smart-cover SW_LID changes release contacts and temporarily
		apply the sensor state; after 500ms the requested window mode
		is restored. No controller firmware or NVM is changed.

		Invalid modes return EINVAL, bad pointers EFAULT and unsupported
		commands ENOTTY. Transport failures are propagated. Device removal
		makes existing files return ENODEV. Debugging commands are not
		implemented. The device is created with mode
		0600; stock init applies its system:radio 0660 permissions.

What:           /sys/bus/i2c/devices/<bus>-004a/double_tap_to_wake
Date:           October 2026
Description:
                Selects controller-side T93 double-tap wake detection.
                Accepts 0 (disabled, default) or 1 (enabled); reads return
                the selected value followed by a newline. Invalid input
                returns EINVAL; changes during system suspend return EBUSY.

                When enabled and the display is blank, T100 and T15 normal
                input reports are disabled, T93 reporting remains enabled,
                and T7 idle/active scan intervals are 64/32 ms. The IRQ
                is armed as a wake source. A T93 double-tap (status bit 1)
                generates one KEY_WAKEUP press/release per blank period.
                There is no host-side tap timer or continuous touch input
                processing. The selected stock T93 thresholds are retained.

                A closed smart cover disables gesture scanning. Returning
                to the display restores the full selected stock RAM touch
                configuration. Disabling uses the existing deep sleep path.
                A failed change restores the previous setting; hardware
                errors are propagated. No firmware or NVM is written.
                The controller and IRQ remain active for gesture detection,
                so power consumption can exceed that of disabled deep sleep.
